now we were talking earlier with the  health secretary about coronavirus and  joining us now to give his take is the  chair at the British Medical Association  dr. John dat nag Paul thank you so much  for being with us it feels to me as if  people are trying to work out how  concerned they should be about  coronavirus how worried should we be  well clearly this is an issue of both  national and international concern so we  do need to be concerned we need to be  vigilant but equally proportionate in  our concern and not panic  or be alarmist we've seen some of the  government's ideas for a battle plan if  you like against coronavirus we spoke to  the health secretary Matt Hancock  earlier on the program he seemed to be  saying look the best thing you can do at  the minute is wash your hands knees into  your elbow etc and is that enough or  should we actually be looking at more  precautions such as the ones that other  countries are taking banning flights for  example from people who recently coming  from China banning meetings of over  5,000 people indoors are we doing enough  so each nation has to take its own  measures based upon circumstances in the  UK we've tested over 10,000 patients or  individuals and we have 23 confirmed  cases so at the moment and of course  this changes on an hour to hour day by  day basis at the moment the containment  seems to be the correct procedure and  therefore simple public health measures  like washing your hands for 20 seconds  making sure that you cover your nose in  a tissue if you sneeze or cough our  proven ways of containing spread of the  virus is another thing else that people  should be doing to keep themselves safe  I mean I was struck by the Dutch the  World Health Organization saying if  you're on over 60 perhaps think about  avoiding crowded places or places where  you could meet people who are sick I  mean it's that kind of self-preservation  if you like that some people should be  thinking about well we know that the  virus is relatively mild in healthy  adults and is much more potent and  causes more illness in those who are  older those with pre-existing medical  conditions and that's advice we give in  fact during the winter season anyway  we're not at that point at the moment  but of course again that advice which is  based around public health experts may  change on a day to day basis but we're  not at that point at the moment how  about traveling I mean if you were  someone over 60 would you be thinking  about going to Italy for example again  we have to be proportionate we have very  up-to-date advice on foreign travel so  anyone considering traveling abroad  should take that advice so again it's  about being proportioned and also making  sure that you seek the right advice it  feels as if caution or in proportion not  panicking it is the message coming  across from oneself and also from the  government given how quickly the virus  seems to have gripped certain countries  after entering them is there not I'm  worried that this is a little bit too  slow that we should actually be doing  things more quickly before we get to  that stage so absolutely whilst we are  at the moment focusing on containment at  this very moment I'm and for the last  few days of not weeks I would be hoping  and I believe there are plans that  should be made regarding an outbreak  that becomes more prevalent well  obviously we need to have emergency  planning for right up to worst-case  scenarios that has to be planned for now  in case that needs to be invoked that's  not been alarmist that's just being  sensible and that is what I believe the  government should be doing and are doing  what is that then well if we were to  have an epidemic in this country they  would need to be we need to move beyond  the containment phase to actually  mitigating or managing that crisis that  may involve for example having to reduce  or cancel planned emergency planned care  in hospitals for non-emergency  operations so that you prioritize the  availability of care for critically ill  patients so that's a sort of thing that  may need to happen but I don't want to  create alarm at this stage that those  discussions that planning will need to  be taking place now and I believe has  been doing so I'm interested  your thoughts and how prepared the  Health Service is for this kind of event  and a couple weeks ago you said this  about the NHS not of course in relation  to coronavirus stories of patient deaths  on corridors round emergency departments  and cancellations of patients procedures  as a daily occurrence are becoming the  new norm I mean could I out service cope  if coronavirus does become more serious  so I think this is the the difficulty  whilst we are rightly preparing for an  outbreak of coronavirus and trying to  contain the virus before this current  episode we had a Health Service in  December that was suffering record waits  for patients in casualty in emergency  departments record numbers of patients  on waiting lists and we heard from the  Royal College of Nursing that 75% of  nurses are now providing corridor care  so we have a Health Service already  under severe pressure and strain are  there enough beds frankly well I think  what would need to happen is that the  nature of the beds would need to change  you probably have to convert parts of  hospitals and wards from routine  services to emergency services or  critical care services these things  would have to be cancelled look if there  was going to be an outbreak or an  epidemic in this country on the  background of a Health Service that was  struggling to cope as the beam the BMA  have said as I've warned as we have the  patients in the in the nation experience  clearly they are going to be  consequences but and the right decisions  will need to be made and in an outbreak  in an epidemic those who are seriously  ill will need to be prioritized and  those plans would be I believe be  happening now because we do need to plan  for the future I mean John McDonald was  saying earlier that austerity on the NHS  could potentially hit how the  government's can respond to this kind of  thing is that right Matt Hancock said it  was not right we've been saying for a  long time  and not just us but all health experts  that we haven't an NHS that has the  sufficient numbers of beds  and staff compared to many other  European countries and we know for  example with when you look at critical  care beds Germany has four times as many  as we have so we clearly have a  background of not the sort of capacity  that the health service needs but having  said that in an emergency situation we  would have to adapt and make provision  for that emergency that faces us just  finally there's been lots of talk about  a vaccine how far away are we I'm  getting a corona virus vaccine so at the  moment a vaccine is being researched the  trouble is that it will take months for  that vaccine to actually be available  and that would be not in time for the  current situation we face so the the the  efforts that we're making now around  identification isolation containment it  is absolutely right and there are other  very practical issues that that we need  to take forward making sure that no one  who may be suspected of how carrying the  infection goes into a GP surgery you  know we've had cases where a patient may  have attended for example to get a fit  note for work and the practice has had  to close down for the day and those  sorts of issues can make a big  difference so it's really important that  the public understand the need to  isolate the need to seek advice from NHS  one-one-one we may need to move to more  remote working for example many GP  surgeries may be offering more telephone  appointments follow-up for patients on  the phone we may need to move to more  social distancing for more patients and  more workers working from home to try  and contain spread of the virus okay  thank you so much for coming on the  program I'd explained they liked it as  much appreciated thank you

